WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:19.170
Robert Hooke: The Leonardo of England
Without a doubt, Robert Hooke was one of the

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.730
greatest minds in British history.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:26.419
His scientific contributions spanned physics,
astronomy, horology, microscopy, paleontology,

00:00:26.419 --> 00:00:27.419
and even architecture.

00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:32.219
And yet, he is nowhere near as well-known
today as his peers.

00:00:32.219 --> 00:00:36.180
The Royal Society believes that Hooke’s
scientific career can be split into three

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.180
parts.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.230
First were the early years when Hooke had
the inquisitive mind and the ambitions necessary

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.000
to become a great scientist.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.890
Then came the apex of his profession around
the time of the Great Fire of London in 1666.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:52.850
His architectural work redesigning and reconstructing
many buildings devastated by the fire brought

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.040
him great wealth.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:59.430
And, lastly, there is the third stage of Hooke’s
life where bitterness and rivalries with other

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:05.049
scientists overshadowed his contributions
and ensured that he became a footnote of history.

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:09.140
But Robert Hooke wasn’t forgotten completely
and today we take a look at the man sometimes

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:14.320
described as the “Leonardo da Vinci of England.”

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:21.130
Early Life
Robert Hooke was born on July 28 (or July

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:26.920
18, going by the Old Style British calendar),
1635, in the village of Freshwater on the

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.420
Isle of Wight, the youngest of four children
to John Hooke and Cecily Gyles.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.810
For the first part of his life, Hooke was
a frail and weak child and his parents had

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.439
little hope that he would survive.

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:40.250
Even so, his father took the time to provide
him with an education.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.840
John Hooke was a priest with the Church of
England at Freshwater’s Church of All Saints.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.520
He had two or three brothers who were also
ministers and expected young Robert to follow

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:49.520
in their footsteps.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.479
From an early age, Robert Hooke showed his
family that he had other interests.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:58.100
He was fascinated with mechanical devices
and toys and enjoyed taking them apart and

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.860
putting them back together again.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.570
Still a child, Hooke built a ship about a
yard long which had proper rigging and could

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.750
sail and even had some small guns that would
fire.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:10.770
On another occasion, he completely disassembled
an old brass clock to see how it worked and

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.380
created a functioning replica out of wood.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.540
Also during childhood, Hooke picked up another
interest that would serve him well in life

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:18.720
- drawing.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.200
The story comes to us courtesy of the book
Brief Lives by 17th century writer and natural

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.840
philosopher John Aubrey, one of the main sources
for Hooke’s early years.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.310
A painter named John Hoskyns came to Freshwater
to draw pictures for an unspecified nobleman.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:36.140
Young Hooke saw him in action and wanted to
give it a shot.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.730
He did not have the right supplies so he had
to improvise.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:43.330
Robert collected chalk, coal, and a red pigment
called ruddle, ground them together and placed

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.090
the mixture on a wooden platter.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:53.600
He then took a pencil and began copying the
pictures that the family had around the house.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.320
Education
Even though it became clear that Robert would

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:02.500
not go down the path of the church like his
father and uncles, John Hooke still wanted

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.030
to ensure that his son had the means to pursue
his profession of choice.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.030
When John Hooke died in 1648, he left Robert
an inheritance of either forty or one hundred

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.030
pounds, depending on the source, so he could
secure an apprenticeship.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.180
At first, it seemed like Hooke was destined
to be an artist.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.210
He became an apprentice to Sir Peter Lely,
a Dutch painter who was one of the premiere

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.050
portraitists of his time.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.600
He also studied with Samuel Cowper, a man
described by Aubrey as the “prince of limners

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:20.650
of this age.”

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.300
A limner was someone who illuminated manuscripts
i.e. decorated them with drawings, borders,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:26.300
and initials.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.580
Hooke’s foray into the art world did not
last long.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:32.110
He discovered that the smell of oil colors
exacerbated the headaches that he suffered

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:37.260
from since childhood so, instead, he enrolled
at the Westminster School in London under

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.910
headmaster Richard Busby.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:42.510
Here, Hooke learned Latin and Greek and got
his first taste of geometry.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:45.910
Early Years at Oxford
Always looking to expand his horizons, Hooke

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:49.790
also took an interest in music and had twenty
lessons on the organ.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:53.870
This was enough to secure him a spot as a
chorister at Christ Church College in Oxford

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:54.870
around 1653.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.900
There, he came under the influence of some
of the most accomplished scientists of that

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.860
era: Robert Boyle, Dr Thomas Willis, John
Wilkins, Christopher Wren.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.990
Eventually, he enrolled to study at Wadham
College.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:09.290
Hooke himself later described these Oxford
days as the period of his life which cemented

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:10.920
his love of science.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.360
We do have limited knowledge of Hooke’s
scientific contributions during these early

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.360
stages.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.350
He served as a chemistry assistant, first
to Willis and then to Boyle.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:23.820
According to his journal, Hooke designed and
built an air pump which Boyle used in his

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.030
experiments with the pneumatic engine.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:29.400
He also came up with a variety of designs
which are lost to history regarding “the

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.400
art of flying.”

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.370
He presented these to John Wilkins who found
them impractical, but ingenious.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:39.180
Hooke’s most significant invention at this
time, though, had to be the anchor escapement.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.820
This was a small cog fitted to pendulum clocks
which helped maintain the swing and made the

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:47.460
clock more accurate by giving a small push
with each swing.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:52.600
Hooke continued his studies of timepieces
as the years went on and, around 1660, also

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.830
created the balance spring for pocket watches.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.160
It should be mentioned, though, that some
believe that credit for this invention is

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.700
due to Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:05.150
At the same time, some historians argue that
Hooke deserves partial recognition for Boyle’s

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:09.700
Law, an experimental gas law described and
named by his employer at the time, Robert

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:10.700
Boyle.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:14.530
The reasoning behind this is that Hooke designed
and built most of the apparatus used in Boyle’s

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:15.530
experiments.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:23.880
The Curator of Experiments at the Royal Society
Something important happened in 1660 - the

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.440
Royal Society was born.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.500
One of the oldest learned societies still
in existence today, it eventually became the

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.300
first national scientific society in the world.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.600
Unofficially, it existed for a couple of decades
prior to it becoming a formal organization

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.750
and receiving a royal charter from King Charles
II.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:45.460
Initially, it consisted of various scientists
who got together to discuss ideas and experiments.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:50.020
Most were part of the Gresham College group
who, as the name implies, met at Gresham College

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.020
in London.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.270
Eventually, twelve of these scholars formed
a committee and founded the Royal Society

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:57.120
of London for Improving Natural Knowledge.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.220
This was a bit of a mouthful, though, so,
by the time the king granted them their charter

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:07.170
in 1662, they were already known simply as
the Royal Society with William Brouncker serving

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:08.280
as their first president.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.080
Now, Robert Hooke was not among the original
twelve founders, but some of his friends and

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.950
mentors were such as Robert Boyle, Christopher
Wren, and John Wilkins.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:21.300
Therefore, it wasn’t surprising that Hooke
was among the first new additions to the club.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.610
In November 1662, he became Curator of Experiments
at the Royal Society.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:31.340
This was described as a position that only
Hooke could fill as he was required to demonstrate

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:36.170
three or four experiments at every meeting
and, at first, he wasn’t even paid to do

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:37.170
it.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:41.280
Yet Hooke acquitted himself marvelously of
his duties as he finally had a scientific

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.980
outlet for all the ideas he accrued over the
years and, as an audience, he had his most

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:50.590
brilliant peers who were in the best position
to judge and appreciate his work.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:54.900
Hooke’s contributions were one of the main
reasons why the society flourished during

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.200
its early stages.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:00.230
Consequently, he was appointed Curator for
life and, also, was granted another position

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:05.190
of Cutlerian Lecturer in the Mechanical Arts
so he may receive another salary from funds

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.340
provided by Sir John Cutler.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:13.280
And if this didn’t keep Hooke busy enough,
in 1665 he also became the Professor of Geometry

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:55.590
at Gresham College.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:08:38.960
Micrographia
Among the many accolades we can ascribe to

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:43.200
Robert Hooke, we can add the popularization
of microscopes to the list.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:49.089
In 1665, he published the first major work
in history on microscopy titled Micrographia

00:08:49.089 --> 00:08:53.800
or, to give it its original, full name, Micrographia:
or Some Physiological Descriptions of Minute

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.580
Bodies Made by Magnifying Glasses.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:58.180
With Observations and Inquiries Thereupon.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:02.520
Even though the title was a tad long-winded,
the book was a hit.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.580
It showed the world that Robert Hooke was
one of the preeminent scholars of his time

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:11.950
and it also helped solidify the status of
the Royal Society as this was its first major

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:12.950
publication.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:17.580
The politician and famed diarist Samuel Pepys
labeled Micrographia “the most ingenious

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.790
book that ever I read in my life.”

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:24.210
Inside the book, people could see for the
first time close-up, detailed drawings of

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:28.870
insects and plants which were done by Hooke
himself, therefore giving us a glimpse at

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:30.930
his skill as an artist, as well.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:34.360
The images were accompanied by insightful,
somewhat artful descriptions.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:39.190
Hooke’s most famous illustration, the flea,
for example, was described as being a “little

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:44.140
busie Creature...adorn’d with a curiously
polish’d suit of sable Armour...and multitudes

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:48.310
of sharp pinns, shap’d almost like Porcupine’s
Quills.”

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:51.700
Some entries were peppered with humor such
as the instance where Hooke described how

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:56.080
he got an ant drunk so that it may sit still
and allowed to be examined.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:01.270
The scientist claimed he gave the insect brandy
which “knock’d him down dead drunk, so

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:04.820
that he became moveless”, only to revive
and run away after about an hour.

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:09.730
It wouldn’t be completely out of the question
to regard Hooke and his Micrographia as a

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:14.540
science popularizer, trying to bring knowledge
to the masses a few centuries before his time.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:18.080
The microscope used for these examinations
was notable in of itself.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.510
Hooke did not build it.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:23.920
He outsourced this task to a famed London
instrument maker named Christopher Cock.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:28.700
Hooke did modify and refine it, though, by
attaching a screw-operated focusing mechanism

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:32.930
of his own design, as well as adding a water
lens which could aim a bright pinpoint of

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:35.149
light on the smallest of targets.

00:10:35.149 --> 00:10:40.140
Another thing worthy of mention in Micrographia
was the first use of the biological term “cell”

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:44.180
which denoted the basic structure which all
organisms are made out of.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:48.830
Hooke coined the word while looking at a piece
of bark from a cork tree under his microscope.

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:52.630
He described it as being “perforated and
porous, much like a honeycomb.”

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:56.730
It was made out of little boxes which Hooke
called “cells” because they reminded him

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.620
of the rooms of monks inside a monastery.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:03.500
Micrographia was a perfect example of how
Hooke’s mind worked.

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:07.279
He was a man of many interests and he developed
them all at the same time.

00:11:07.279 --> 00:11:10.080
He wasn’t one to focus his attention on
just one thing.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:15.020
Even though the book was intended as a presentation
of microscopy, it also described distant planetary

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.580
bodies which Hooke observed with his telescope.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:22.330
It also presented his examination of fossilized
wood and Hooke’s associated ideas which

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:27.410
resembled an early version of the . He also
talked about the wave theory of light and

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:32.330
other scientific interests which had nothing
to do with the original purpose of Micrographia.

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:34.420
Hooke also mentioned his ideas on combustion.

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:39.490
At a time when other scientists were adopting
the theory of phlogiston, a fire-like component

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:43.720
which they thought existed in combustible
elements, Hooke opined that the process was

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:47.210
determined by a substance which was mixed
with air.

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:51.040
Although this is pure speculation, some modern
scholars believe that, if Hooke had pursued

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:55.690
this line of inquiry more fervently, he would
have discovered oxygen a hundred years earlier.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:58.230
Robert Hooke, Architect
Although Hooke was, undoubtedly, a scientist,

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:02.340
he actually made more money in his lifetime
from his side job as an architect.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:07.860
On September 2, 1666, London was ravaged by
a great fire which burned down most of the

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:09.279
medieval part of the city.

00:12:09.279 --> 00:12:14.890
The blaze lasted for five days and consumed
over 13,000 structures, most of them houses.

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:17.650
Afterwards came the monumental task of rebuilding
the city.

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:22.490
The duty (and the ensuing accolades) fell
on Christopher Wren, someone who is literally

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:25.370
remembered as “the man who rebuilt London.”

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:27.060
This is not without merit.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:30.360
Wren oversaw the construction of many important
buildings and structures.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:34.970
He designed 52 new churches, including his
magnum opus, St. Paul’s Cathedral.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:39.480
Wren was aided in his endeavor by Robert Hooke
who served as his chief assistant and the

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:41.410
new Surveyor to the City of London.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:46.500
But while Wren’s name is now inexorably
tied to the architectural landscape of London,

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:50.779
Hooke’s contributions are seldom remembered
even though he surveilled and certified nearly

00:12:50.779 --> 00:12:53.870
3,000 new foundations between 1667 and 1672.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:59.350
In his own time, John Aubrey gave Hooke credit
for building the Royal College of Physicians,

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:02.840
Bethlem Royal Hospital, and Montagu House,
among others.

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:07.100
Even he did not know the full extent of Hooke’s
input, merely noting that Hooke was “much

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.970
made use of” when it came to designing new
structures.

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:14.470
It is hard to say with any certainty what
the dynamic was between the partnership of

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:15.800
Wren and Hooke.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:19.550
Some modern historians even argue that Hooke
was the more important partner because it

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:23.510
was his expertise in mechanics which made
many of the new buildings possible.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:28.600
We will probably never know for certain the
extent of the influence that Hooke had on

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:31.120
the landscape of London.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:37.660
Hooke’s Law
Hooke’s lasting contribution to physics

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:40.190
is the law of elasticity that shares his name.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:44.870
Hooke’s Law states that the force needed
to compress or extend a spring by some distance

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:46.899
is proportional to that distance.

00:13:46.899 --> 00:13:53.300
The formula for this principle is F=-kX, where
F is the force applied to the spring, X is

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:58.420
the distance the spring compresses or expands
and k is the spring constant which denotes

00:13:58.420 --> 00:13:59.420
its stiffness.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:03.700
The formula is typically presented with a
negative value to show that the force exerted

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:06.560
by the spring opposes its displacement.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:11.100
What might be more interesting to us, however,
was the way in which Hooke presented his new

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:12.100
principle.

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.959
We are not exactly sure when Hooke first came
up with it.

00:14:13.959 --> 00:14:19.440
He had made claims about developing it as
far back as 1660, but he published it in 1678.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:24.830
Two years prior, however, he gave everyone
a teaser in another book of his titled Descriptions

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:26.100
of Helioscopes.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:30.769
He listed ten new discoveries and inventions
which he intended to present in the near future.

00:14:30.769 --> 00:14:36.060
One of them was “The true Theory of Elasticity
or Springiness, and a particular Explication

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:41.000
thereof in several Subjects in which it is
to be found: And the way of computing the

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:43.120
velocity of Bodies moved by them.

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:44.120
ceiiinosssttuv.”

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:50.770
That last bit was not a typo, by the way,
it was an anagram.

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:56.080
Two years later, he revealed that it meant
“Ut tensio, sic vis” which was Latin for

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:58.440
“as the extension, so the force.”

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:01.970
You might consider it a bit odd that Hooke
went to such lengths, but it was not completely

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:07.080
unheard of in those days to conceal your workings
behind anagrams, cyphers, or some other kind

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:08.160
of hidden message.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.380
The idea was that, by publicly presenting
a theory in this way, the scientist established

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:14.800
priority without revealing too much information.

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:19.430
Afterwards, they were free to keep working
on their project for months, years, even decades,

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:22.490
until it was fully fleshed out and ready to
present to the world.

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:27.440
If, in the meantime, another researcher unveiled
the same idea, the initial scientist had proof

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:30.200
that they started work on the subject first.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:34.250
There are quite a few important discoveries
and inventions whose originators are under

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.810
doubt because multiple scientists worked on
them at the same time.

00:15:37.810 --> 00:15:42.061
Hence, Hooke’s use of the anagram, a strategy
that had been employed by other prominent

00:15:42.061 --> 00:15:43.430
scientists such as Newton, Galileo, or Huygens.

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:52.100
A Rivalry with Newton
As you can see, Robert Hooke was incredibly

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:57.420
accomplished, yet he enjoys only a fraction
of the renown of his great contemporaries.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:58.900
But why is that?

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:02.610
You know the old saying “history is written
by the victor?”

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:06.640
In this case, it could simply be that Hooke
picked a fight with the wrong guy.

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:10.100
Setting aside Hooke’s many talents, it is
important to remember that he was still a

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:11.720
man full of foibles.

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:15.410
It seems that he was particularly prickly
when he perceived that others tried to take

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:17.120
credit for something he came up with.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:21.010
The problem was that Hooke often moved on
from one idea to another if he did not perceive

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:24.760
it to be important enough or something he
could use to patent and profit.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:28.720
This meant that a lot of his notions were
undeveloped and never advanced past the stage

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:30.700
of scribbles on a piece of paper.

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:34.760
It would be a little like crediting da Vinci
with the flying machine because he made a

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:35.960
drawing of one.

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:39.640
Unsurprisingly, this attitude led to several
feuds with fellow scholars.

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:43.540
We already mentioned in passing that he and
Christiaan Huygens argued over who developed

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:45.510
a spring for watches first.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:49.920
He also quarreled with a German theologian
and scientist named Henry Oldenburg.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:54.390
But Hooke’s greatest rivalry, one that had
a profound effect on his reputation, was with

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:55.390
Isaac Newton.

00:16:55.390 --> 00:17:00.540
The relationship between the two turned sour
in 1672 when Newton presented his notions

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:01.820
on light and color.

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:06.949
Hooke insisted that Newton’s work was based
on ideas of his which he developed in 1665.

00:17:06.949 --> 00:17:11.559
This quarrel was only the appetizer, though,
as the main course came in 1687 when Isaac

00:17:11.559 --> 00:17:17.470
Newton published Philosophiae Naturalis Principia
Mathematica or, simply, the Principia, arguably

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.379
the most famous book in the history of science.

00:17:20.379 --> 00:17:25.119
Among other seminal principles, the Principia
contained Newton’s law of universal gravitation,

00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:28.199
an inverse square law that showed how planetary
motion worked.

00:17:28.199 --> 00:17:32.309
It was a landmark moment in physics, but Hooke
accused Newton of plagiarism.

00:17:32.309 --> 00:17:36.350
It is actually pretty hard to establish how
much credit, if any, should go to Hooke.

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:40.530
By Newton’s own admission, he corresponded
with Hooke on the matter, but he maintained

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:42.770
that the latter never provided any new information.

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:46.769
He might have concluded that an inverse square
law governed the motion of the planets, but

00:17:46.769 --> 00:17:50.679
he never produced any proof for it even though
he claimed to have it.

00:17:50.679 --> 00:17:54.980
This point of view was corroborated by Edmond
Halley, another esteemed scientist known today

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:55.980
for Halley’s Comet.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:18:00.399
He said that he asked for proof from both
Hooke and Newton, but only the latter delivered.

00:18:00.399 --> 00:18:04.679
Halley later encouraged Newton to write the
Principia and financed its publication out

00:18:04.679 --> 00:18:05.820
of his own pocket.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:09.919
French astronomer Alexis Clairaut was more
tactful when dismissing Hooke’s claims,

00:18:09.919 --> 00:18:14.470
saying that they showed us “what a distance
there is between a truth that is glimpsed

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:16.279
and a truth that is demonstrated.”

00:18:16.279 --> 00:18:23.279
What Did Hooke Look Like?

00:18:23.279 --> 00:18:26.779
Hooke’s rivalry with Newton had a negative
effect on his image, as well as his standing

00:18:26.779 --> 00:18:27.830
in the scientific world.

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:31.820
Some of his later lectures reveal that he
never conceded the point that he came up with

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:33.440
the inverse square law first.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:38.019
In one of those lectures, he said “...concerning
those properties of gravity which I myself

00:18:38.019 --> 00:18:43.249
first discovered and showed to this Society
and years since, which of late Mr Newton has

00:18:43.249 --> 00:18:47.619
done me the favour to print and publish as
his own inventions.”

00:18:47.619 --> 00:18:50.350
Hooke gained a reputation of being bitter
and disgruntled.

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:55.580
He died in 1703 of an unspecified illness
and was buried at St Helen’s Bishopsgate,

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:57.369
although the exact location is unknown.

00:18:57.369 --> 00:19:01.730
Soon afterwards, another Royal Society member
named Richard Waller published a collection

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:05.610
of Hooke’s posthumous works and included
a short biography where he described him as

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:08.950
being “melancholy, mistrustful, and jealous.”

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:14.000
This biography, the only one from a contemporary,
influenced all those that followed, but we

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.230
do not know the relationship between Hooke
and Waller.

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:20.739
As for Newton, he became President of the
Royal Society the same year that Hooke died

00:19:20.739 --> 00:19:25.929
and it is said that he took steps to ensure
that the world would forget his former rival.

00:19:25.929 --> 00:19:30.119
This may have included the destruction of
the only known portrait of Robert Hooke so

00:19:30.119 --> 00:19:32.880
that even his likeness would be lost to history.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:36.289
But before we go on besmirching Newton’s
good name, we should stress that there is

00:19:36.289 --> 00:19:41.129
no solid evidence that he ever did this or
even that the portrait existed in the first

00:19:41.129 --> 00:19:43.299
place other than a few mentions.

00:19:43.299 --> 00:19:47.520
Some have speculated that Hooke never wanted
to sit for a portrait because he had a weak

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.869
and misshapen body due to his childhood illness.

00:19:49.869 --> 00:19:52.669
Indeed, today we have no idea what he looked
like.

00:19:52.669 --> 00:19:56.889
This is the most famous representation of
Hooke based on the few available descriptions,

00:19:56.889 --> 00:20:01.679
but you might be surprised to learn that this
painting, done by Rita Greer, is only 15 years

00:20:01.679 --> 00:20:02.679
old.

00:20:02.679 --> 00:20:05.820
She is one of the people who, in recent years,
have undertaken efforts to restore Robert

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:25.539
Hooke to his rightful place in the pantheon
of Britain’s greatest scholars.

